\p{The Cedar slice at 8 workers on the launcher did not reach a steady state in 7 passes (K1's own two runs: 398-928 ms) and is not in the table.}

\p{\strong{Table B - the promotion policy} (earlier windows, before C7 / C8, machine jar \code{\verb~|4bc20d3e46ef~}; C7 keeps one machine per session reachable in the tier runs, so the 8-worker rows are biased against the tier by an amount I did not separate; they are here for the comparison of the policies with each other):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Workload}\th{Platform}\th{Workers}\th{K1}\th{tier, at 1000 entries, unbounded}\th{tier, at 20000 entries, at most 96 blocks}\th{windows}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{JVM}\td{1}\td{1015 (4 runs: 986-1058)}\td{973 (-4.2\%; 212 blocks)}\td{949 (-6.5\%; 26 blocks)}\td{J6}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{JVM}\td{8}\td{520}\td{529 (+1.7\%; 177 blocks)}\td{-}\td{J4}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{native}\td{1}\td{1475 (2 runs)}\td{1370 against 1424 in its window (-3.8\%; 252 blocks)}\td{1392 (-5.6\%; 26 blocks)}\td{N3, N2}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{native}\td{8}\td{761 (2 runs)}\td{779 (+2.3\%; 182 blocks)}\td{707 (-7.1\%; 28 blocks)}\td{N3}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice}\td{JVM}\td{1}\td{1359 (2 runs)}\td{1489 against 1438 in its window (+3.5\%; 804 blocks)}\td{1462 (+7.6\%; 96 blocks)}\td{J6, J3}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice}\td{JVM}\td{8}\td{232}\td{321 (+38\%; 686 blocks)}\td{-}\td{J4}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice}\td{native}\td{1}\td{1869}\td{-}\td{1888 (+1.0\%; 88 blocks)}\td{N3}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice}\td{native}\td{8}\td{319}\td{391 (+23\%; 713 blocks)}\td{351 (+10\%; 96 blocks)}\td{N3}}
\tr{\td{full cedar}\td{JVM}\td{8}\td{1315}\td{1827 (+39\%; 1,820 blocks, 52.6 s of compilation)}\td{-}\td{J4}}
}

\p{Compilation: a block takes about 30 ms of compiler CPU on the JVM (7.6 s for 252 blocks of reachability, 27 s for 804 of the Cedar slice) and about 20 ms in the image (5.4 s for 252). Deoptimisations: 0 in every measured run (the nodes have no profile and no assumption; \code{\verb~|engine.MaximumCompilations=1~}). Failed compilations: 0 in every run of the tier without the run-ahead; with it 1 of 804, 2 of 692 and 6 of 1,821 blocks (they stay in the stepper).}

\p{What the numbers say:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Closed evaluation gains, and only with few blocks.} Reachability is 83\% inside code blocks and three blocks have two thirds of its 2.5 M activations per pass. Compiling the 11-14 blocks that pass the final policy gives -7.5 to -10\% on the JVM (WarmMain and the launcher, 8 workers) and -3.8 to -4.8\% in the image; 26 blocks gave -5.6 to -7.1\% (both platforms, table B); 180-250 blocks give less (-4\%) or nothing (8 workers: +2\%). The ceiling is the stepper's own share of such a run, 17\% (section 5); the rest is interning, which compiled code repeats call for call. One row does not fit: the launcher at one worker (-1.5\%), one run.}
\li{\strong{Cedar-level work has nothing to gain and, on the JVM, something to lose.} The Cedar slice spends 14\% of its time inside blocks at all, spread over hundreds of them. With the final policy two blocks are compiled, 45 k compiled activations per pass. In the image that is neutral (-0.3\% at one worker, +2.4\% at eight, inside the scatter). On the JVM the pass is 60 ms slower at one worker (+4.3\%) and at eight (+30\% of 220 ms, at unchanged CPU time of the workers): more than 1 microsecond per compiled activation, which compiled code cannot cost. C7 and C8 were found while looking for the cause and are not it (J7 before them: +27\% with one block); section 9, point 3 has what I know. With an unbounded count everything is compiled in the end, because the count is cumulative: 804 blocks on the slice, 1,820 on full cedar, the compiler threads busy through every measured pass and, with 8 workers on 8 cores, taking cores from them: +23 to +39\% (table B).}
\li{\strong{T2's "55-80\% loss on the native image"} was the heat accounting and the measurement, not the code (section 3).}
\li{\strong{Platform}: K1 itself is 20-30\% faster on the JVM than in the native image in steady state, same machine jar (\code{\verb~|4bc20d3e46ef~}): reachability 1.02 s against 1.42-1.48 s, Cedar slice 1.36-1.44 s against 1.78-1.87 s (one worker), Cedar slice with 8 workers 232 ms against 298-319 ms. That is not a tier result, but under OWNER-NOTES 12 it is the larger number in this file. (WarmMain, not the driver: no loading, no admission pipeline.)}
}

\p{\strong{Promotion policy} (what \code{\verb~|mode=warm~} now does; \code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.at~}, \code{\verb~|.hot~}, \code{\verb~|.max~}): the tier counts, per machine, the activations it does not take; a block is promoted when the engine has seen 20000 of them AND the block has at least 2\% of ALL activations of the engine so far (compiled ones included), at most 256 blocks; the machine's heat accounting is never switched on; two compiler threads. In a warm-up-is-free world the threshold could be lower - but a cumulative count below "this block carries the run" is what produces table B's Cedar rows, so the test that matters is the share, not the count.}

\p{\strong{If the integrator wants it on for an evaluation-heavy run anyway}: \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,code,casetrees,t2 -Drlvm.t2.mode=warm~}, with \code{\verb~|rlvm-truffle~}'s jar on the class path (module not in the parent pom: README "Build, test"); with \code{\verb~|-X passes=N -X passes-mode=recheck -X passes-keep=engines~} (rlvm-driver \code{\verb~|150aed4bf1bf~}) the launcher shows it.}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. How the numbers were taken}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Tool}: \code{\verb~|rlvm.truffle.WarmMain~} (test tree): one process, ONE machine engine kept over all passes; pass 1 cold with the environment pass, every later pass checks all units again with fresh sessions of the same engine on W worker threads (chunks of 16 units from a shared counter). This is what "warm" has to mean for this tier: until 18:04Z the launcher's \code{\verb~|-X passes=N -X passes-mode=recheck~} built new engines per pass (T2's finding of 16:20Z), so no launcher run could credit a code store or compiled blocks. Not the driver: no admission pipeline, no re-check, no pipelined loading.}
\li{\strong{Launcher rows} (label L1, \code{\verb~|work/t3/launchrun.py~}, \code{\verb~|launcher.tsv~}): \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm --threads W FILE -X engine=machine -X passes=N -X passes-mode=recheck -X passes-keep=engines~} with rlvm-driver \code{\verb~|150aed4bf1bf~} (P7, 18:04Z): the real driver, the engine instances of pass 1 kept; the number read is \code{\verb~|check_ms~} of the later passes.}
\li{\strong{Quiet}: every number in a table below comes from inside \code{\verb~|bench/quiet.py -- work/t3/wN.sh~} (one measurement at a time on the 8 reserved performance cores; \code{\verb~|quiet.log~} has each window). Runs of one window are back to back; a configuration is repeated inside the window where the table says so. Rows: \code{\verb~|work/t3/warm.tsv~} (column \code{\verb~|quiet~} = the window), complete outputs \code{\verb~|work/t3/logs/~}.}
\li{\strong{What is read}: warm WALL clock per pass (OWNER-NOTES 12). With one worker the worker thread's CPU time is within 1-3\% of it; compiler threads run on the other measurement cores.}
\li{\strong{Platforms}: JVM = the launcher's flags (\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2~}, C2 for the host, libgraal for Truffle), \code{\verb~|-Xmx6g~}; native = CE native image \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~} of \code{\verb~|WarmMain~} (\code{\verb~|work/t3/build-dist.sh --warm~}).}
\li{\strong{K1} = \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,code,casetrees~} (the default set of 16:24Z plus the K1 blocks and case trees); \strong{K1 + t2} = the same plus \code{\verb~|t2~}.}
\li{\strong{Machine jars}: \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~} is republished every few minutes. A distribution of mine is a snapshot (\code{\verb~|BUILD-INFO.txt~}); K1 and K1 + t2 of one table row are always the same distribution, rows of different distributions are not compared.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. T2's tier as it was left: parity on the JVM, a small loss on the native image, and why}

\p{Warm wall clock per pass in ms (median of the later passes; in brackets the range of all warm passes); one worker unless stated; \code{\verb~|quiet.log~} windows of 16:46Z, 17:00Z (JVM, distribution \code{\verb~|work/t3/j1~}, machine jar \code{\verb~|a7e37a04cf90~}) and 17:09Z (native, \code{\verb~|work/t3/n1~}, machine jar \code{\verb~|ba557722c184~}).}

\table{
\tr{\th{Workload, platform}\th{K1}\th{K1 + t2, as T2 left it (\code{\verb~|mode=warm~}, heat timing 4096)}\th{K1 + t2, promote at 2000 entries, heat counting only}\th{tier attached, NOTHING promoted, heat counting only}\th{the same, heat timing 4096}}
\tr{\td{reachability, JVM (window 1)}\td{1027 / 980 (962-1103)}\td{1031 / 971 (910-1061)}\td{-}\td{-}\td{1138 (1095-1187)}}
\tr{\td{reachability, JVM (window 2)}\td{1049 (993-1113)}\td{1023 (980-1095)}\td{1023 (970-1070)}\td{1074 (1052-1190)}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{reachability, JVM, 8 workers}\td{552 (496-623)}\td{-}\td{552 (496-699)}\td{-}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice, JVM}\td{1443 (1369-1559)}\td{-}\td{1505 (1407-1581)}\td{-}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{reachability, native}\td{1399 (1358-1501)}\td{1449 (1393-1475)}\td{1445 (1356-1480)}\td{1470 (1454-1490)}\td{1518 (1457-1617)}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice, native}\td{1844 (1838-1851)}\td{-}\td{1879 (1843-1934)}\td{-}\td{-}}
}

\p{Blocks installed / compile CPU / compiled activations per warm pass: reachability 105-148 blocks, 2.0-2.7 s, 2.5 M (JVM) and 107-138, 1.7-2.1 s, 2.6 M (native); Cedar slice 576 blocks, 15.5 s, 0.88 M (JVM) and 468, 14.1 s, 0.87 M (native) - on the Cedar slice blocks were still being promoted in the last measured pass. Deoptimisations: 0 everywhere.}

\p{What the table says:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The machine's heat accounting is most of what the tier costs.} Counting every entry (\code{\verb~|Tier.count~}): +2.4\% (JVM), +5.0\% (native). Timing on top, even at one sampled entry in 4096 (a sampled entry times every block entered beneath it): +11\% (JVM), +8.5\% (native) against K1. T2's tier switched it on because its governor rents by measured time.}
\li{\strong{Compiled blocks win back about that much and no more}: against "tier attached, nothing promoted" they gain 4.7\% (JVM) and 1.7\% (native) on reachability. Net against K1: -2.5\% to parity on the JVM, +3.3\% on the native image, +2 to +4\% on the Cedar slice.}
\li{\strong{The cause of T2's "55-80\% loss on the native image"} (its row Tn1): not the compiled code and not a deoptimisation loop. In a quiet window the tier costs 3-4\% there (points 1 and 2). T2's row was process CPU on the open host with the heat timing at 64 or 4096: it adds the compiler threads (0.25-0.4 s per pass while blocks are still promoted: process CPU 1640-1870 ms against 1392-1480 ms of wall clock in the same passes here) and the host's own factor of 1.5-2 between runs minutes apart.}
\li{The JVM's K1 is faster than the image's in these windows too (1.03-1.05 s against 1.40 s on reachability), but the two distributions hold different machine jars; the same-jar comparison is section 1, point 4.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. What was changed in the tier (all inside \code{\verb~|rlvm-truffle~}; nothing in \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~})}

\table{
\tr{\th{\verb~|#~}\th{Change}\th{Where}\th{State}}
\tr{\td{C1}\td{\strong{Promotion by the tier's own entry counters, without the machine's heat accounting} (\code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.count~}, default in \code{\verb~|mode=warm~}; policy C9). \code{\verb~|T2Tier.run~} counts the activations it does NOT take (per machine, reported to the engine in batches, C8); an activation that compiled code takes is counted and timed by nobody. \code{\verb~|Tier.heat(engine)~} is not called, so \code{\verb~|Tier.exec~} is a plain call of the tier. \code{\verb~|mode=cpu~} keeps T2's rent-to-own governor on the heat accounting}\td{\code{\verb~|T2Tier~}, \code{\verb~|T2Engine.bump~}, \code{\verb~|Settings~}}\td{kept; removes the 2.4-11\% of section 3}}
\tr{\td{C2}\td{\strong{Pure-read helpers compiled into the block} (\code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.inline~}, a class constant, now 1): \code{\verb~|PEEK~} of an operand, the constructor test of \code{\verb~|SWITCH~}, the argument base of \code{\verb~|FIELDS~}, the inline-cache probe of \code{\verb~|KVAL~}, the cache of CONST heads in front of \code{\verb~|KCONST~}. Everything that can write or grow a store stays a call behind a boundary}\td{\code{\verb~|Instr~}, \code{\verb~|T2Bridge.*Inline / *Hit~}}\td{kept}}
\tr{\td{C3}\td{\strong{\code{\verb~|KCONST~} with level parameters uses the site's inline cache as the stepper does} (\code{\verb~|Code.kConst~}: one probe; miss: \code{\verb~|CHEAD(D, LSUB(LL, ls))~} and fill). T2's node called \code{\verb~|lsubList~} + \code{\verb~|mkConst~} every time (the stepper's cache is of wave 6, NOTES-M7; the mirror did not have it)}\td{\code{\verb~|Instr.Konst~}, \code{\verb~|T2Bridge.kConstHit / kConstFill~}}\td{kept (a drift, not a wrong value)}}
\tr{\td{C4}\td{\strong{Nat literals natively} (owner note 7): \code{\verb~|NSUCC~} of an immediate below the limit is \code{\verb~|v + 1~} in the block (overflow guard: the operation), the predecessor view of \code{\verb~|FIELDS~} is \code{\verb~|v - 1~}, \code{\verb~|SWITCH~} on an immediate is a compare}\td{\code{\verb~|Instr.Unary1 / Fields / Switch~}}\td{kept; the value model already has the immediates, so this removes two calls, not a boxing}}
\tr{\td{C5}\td{Run-ahead of the interning probes (\code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.ahead~}, section 6)}\td{\code{\verb~|BlockRoot.ahead~}, \code{\verb~|Instr.*.ahead~}, \code{\verb~|T2Bridge~}}\td{\strong{rejected by measurement, default off}}}
\tr{\td{C6}\td{No one-call forwarders in code that partial evaluation reaches (\code{\verb~|T2Bridge.peekInline~} and the hash functions are written out; \code{\verb~|T2Bridge.hashOk()~} compares them with the machine's once per engine and switches the inline-cache probes back to the boundary operations if they ever differ)}\td{\code{\verb~|T2Bridge~}}\td{needed for the native image: a method whose body is one call is inlined before the image's analysis and its deoptimisation target is then missing - the rule T2 found for one-store setters holds for forwarders too}}
\tr{\td{C7}\td{\strong{The engine keeps the tiers' counters, not the tiers.} \code{\verb~|T2Engine~} had a list of every \code{\verb~|T2Tier~} it ever made (for the report), a tier references its machine, and an engine outlives its sessions: every machine that ever ran in an engine stayed reachable, with its arenas. With fresh sessions per pass (WarmMain; the driver's kept engines) the heap grew by the workers' arenas each pass, for the tier runs only}\td{\code{\verb~|T2Tier.Counts~}, \code{\verb~|T2Engine.register~}}\td{fixed 18:30Z (a defect since T2's first version). The rows of sections 1 and 3 taken before it (labels J1-J7, N1-N3) carry it; with one worker it is one machine per pass. Rows after it: J8, J9, L1, N5}}
\tr{\td{C8}\td{\strong{Count mode reports per 256 stepped activations of a block} (was 64) and carries the machine's compiled activations along, in one short critical section (before: a second lock and a loop over all tiers at every report of a block above the threshold)}\td{\code{\verb~|T2Tier.miss~}, \code{\verb~|T2Engine.bump~}}\td{fixed 18:34Z; eight workers met in that section often enough to show (section 1, rows J7 against J9)}}
\tr{\td{C9}\td{\strong{Promotion policy of \code{\verb~|mode=warm~}}: at 20000 entries AND 2\% of all activations of the engine (\code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.hot~}), at most 256 blocks (was: at 1000 entries, at most 4096)}\td{\code{\verb~|Settings~}, \code{\verb~|T2Engine.bump~}}\td{section 1}}
\tr{\td{C10}\td{\code{\verb~|T2Engine.retire()~}: an engine whose harness closes cancels its queued compilations (tools and tests with several engines in one process)}\td{\code{\verb~|T2Engine~}, \code{\verb~|T2Runtime.promote~}, \code{\verb~|Harness.close~}}\td{section 8}}
}

\p{Not done, and why: direct calls between compiled blocks (section 7, U1).}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Where the time of a block goes (why removing the dispatch buys so little)}

\p{JFR execution samples of warm K1 passes on the JVM (one worker; open host, so SHARES only; \code{\verb~|work/t3/jfr/*.top.txt~}):}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{reachability}\th{Cedar slice}}
\tr{\td{inside code blocks, inclusive (\code{\verb~|Code.enter~})}\td{83\%}\td{14\%}}
\tr{\td{the stepper's own code (\code{\verb~|Code.*~} self: dispatch, operand decoding, register traffic, \code{\verb~|go~})}\td{16.8\%}\td{6.9\%}}
\tr{\td{value construction BY BLOCK INSTRUCTIONS, inclusive: \code{\verb~|Code.mkApp~} / \code{\verb~|Code.mkClosure~} / \code{\verb~|Code.mkProj~}}\td{42\% / 14\% / 4\%}\td{3\% / 3\% / 0.3\%}}
\tr{\td{self time of the probes and inserts, whoever calls them (\code{\verb~|Values.neu~}, \code{\verb~|closureProbe~}, \code{\verb~|Runs.internRun~}, \code{\verb~|Values.rec~}, \code{\verb~|neuExt~}, \code{\verb~|Machine.addRecordAt~}, \code{\verb~|growVtab~}, \code{\verb~|growRtab~})}\td{57\%}\td{26\%}}
\tr{\td{generic judgments (\code{\verb~|Infer.check~} inclusive)}\td{8\%}\td{59\%}}
}

\p{A compiled block calls the same operations as the stepper (one semantics, the same records in the same order - the tier-equivalence rule). What partial evaluation can remove is the second row; the third and fourth rows are identical work in both tiers. That bounds the gain of ANY compilation of single blocks at 17\% on reachability and 7\% on the Cedar slice before its own entry costs; measured on reachability: 4-10\% (section 1).}

\p{The hottest blocks of reachability (activations per warm pass; \code{\verb~|work/t3/logs/listing-reach-t2-0.log~}):}

\pre{\verb~|
570 k  RULE(List.rec)        BUNREAD A3, 2/3, r1, L1 ; KCONST r0, List.rec ; MKAPP r1, r0, A0, A1, A2, A3, A5 ; L1: TAILAPPLY A3, A4, A5, r1
560 k  BODY(#913)            (entry 3) BKIND C1 .. ; MKSUSP r0 ; MKSUSP r3 ; KCONST ; MKAPP r5 (List.below C0 C1 A1) ; KCONST ; MKAPP r7 (PProd r3 r5) ;
                             MKSUSP r8 ; KCONST ; MKAPP r12 (PProd.mk r0 r7 r8 A2) ; RET r12        - 3 frames + 3 suspensions + 3 applications interned
500 k  RULES(List.elem._f)   WHNF r0, A3 ; SWITCH r0, List ; FIELDS r2, r0, 2 ; KCONST r4, BEq.beq ; MKAPP r5, r4, A0, A1, A2, r2 ; WHNF r6, r5 ; SWITCH r6, Bool ; ..
 40 k  RULES(instDecidableEqFin)  KCONST x3 ; MKAPP r3 (Fin.val A0 A1) ; MKAPP r5 (Fin.val A0 A2) ; MKAPP r7 (decEq Nat inst r3 r5) ; WHNF r8, r7 ; SWITCH ; FIELDS ;
                             MKAPP (Fin A0) ; MKAPP (Eq ..) ; MKAPP (.._proof_1 A0 A1 A2 r9) ; MKAPP (Decidable.isFalse ..) ; RET
~}

\p{A block is "build the graph, then ask a generic judgment to reduce it": the redex \code{\verb~|decEq Nat inst a b~} is an interned record before \code{\verb~|WHNF~} looks at it, the types \code{\verb~|List.below ..~} / \code{\verb~|PProd ..~} and the proof terms are interned although nothing ever inspects them, and the callee is reached through \code{\verb~|Reduce.whnf~} / \code{\verb~|Eval.apply~}, never called. There is no arithmetic and no branch a JIT could specialise beyond what the stepper already does: \code{\verb~|Nat~} literals are immediates in the value model (owner note 7 is satisfied by the machine itself; the tier now adds the native paths for \code{\verb~|NSUCC~} and the predecessor view of \code{\verb~|FIELDS~}).}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Experiment REJECTED: run-ahead of the interning probes (\code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.ahead~}, default 0)}

\p{\strong{Idea.} The cost that dominates a block is interning, and M1 had measured on the generic tier (v0, native, perf annotate; requests.log 15:58Z) that 76-83\% of the samples of a probe are ONE instruction, the load of the table slot of a 64-256 MiB table. A block issues its probes one after the other (each is a call that ends before the next starts, and often needs the previous result: \code{\verb~|MKSUSP~} = the frame's probe, then the record's probe over the frame's id). A compiled block, unlike the stepper, has its whole future in front of it as constants, so it could load the slots EARLY and let the cache misses overlap.}

\p{\strong{As built} (\code{\verb~|BlockRoot.ahead~}, \code{\verb~|Instr.*.ahead~}, \code{\verb~|T2Bridge.neuFind / recordFind / runFind / *Hash~}; reads of the machine only, one store into a field of the tier object to keep the loads; tier equivalence is not touched, and the differential gate of section 8 ran with it on). At an entry of a root, and again after every instruction that called a judgment, the block walks the stretch of value construction that follows with PREDICTED registers: \code{\verb~|MOV~}, \code{\verb~|PEEK~}, \code{\verb~|KCONST~} / \code{\verb~|KVAL~} (cache hits), \code{\verb~|FIELDS~}, \code{\verb~|BKIND~} / \code{\verb~|BEQ~} / \code{\verb~|SWITCH~} on known values, and for \code{\verb~|MKAPP~}, \code{\verb~|MKLAM~}, \code{\verb~|MKPI~}, \code{\verb~|MKSUSP~} the hash of the key exactly as \code{\verb~|Values.neu~} / \code{\verb~|Runs.internRun~} / \code{\verb~|Values.closureProbe~} compute it and the load of the table word where the probe will start. A key that is not in the table is predicted to get the next record id (\code{\verb~|vp~}) or run offset (\code{\verb~|rsp~}), so that the keys built on it can be hashed before it exists. Two forms: "assume new" (never waits for a loaded word) and exact (\code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.aheadExact~}: the loaded word is read - empty = new, the key = the existing record; first four slots of the probe sequence; the frame / application predicted new a moment ago is remembered for sibling closures). \code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.aheadCheck=true~} counts the predictions: on reachability the exact form names the record the operation then returns for 90.3\% of 13.6 M records per run; "assume new" 28\% - HALF of the records that reachability's blocks build exist already.}

\p{\strong{Result} (quiet windows J3 of 17:55Z, JVM, and N2 of 18:00Z, native; promotion at 1000 entries; warm wall clock per pass in ms, median of the later half of the passes of both runs, the two runs' own medians in brackets; the same tier with and without the run-ahead):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Workload}\th{K1}\th{tier, no run-ahead}\th{tier + run-ahead, exact}\th{tier + run-ahead, "assume new"}\th{compile CPU without / with (exact)}}
\tr{\td{reachability, JVM, 1 worker}\td{1069 (1022, 1105)}\td{1021 (1012, 1041)}\td{1116 (1082, 1120)}\td{1147 (1167, 1109)}\td{7.6 s / 36-37 s (252 blocks)}}
\tr{\td{reachability, native, 1 worker}\td{1424 (1408, 1442)}\td{1370 (one run)}\td{1484 (1500, 1462)}\td{-}\td{5.4 s / 14.5 s}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice, JVM, 1 worker (one run each)}\td{1438}\td{1489}\td{1742}\td{-}\td{27 s / 140 s (804 blocks)}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice, native, 1 worker (one run each)}\td{1775}\td{-}\td{2061}\td{-}\td{- / 56 s}}
}

\p{Against the same tier without it, the run-ahead is 8-12\% SLOWER on reachability and far slower on the Cedar slice (where the compiler threads are then busy through every measured pass). JFR on reachability with the exact form (\code{\verb~|work/t3/jfr/t3-reach.top.txt~}; against the K1 profile of section 5): \code{\verb~|Values.neu~} has 15.5\% of the samples instead of 22\%, and the run-ahead itself has 17\% (\code{\verb~|MkApp.ahead~} 10.3\%): the waiting moves into the run-ahead, it does not overlap. Why, as far as I can say (readings, not measurements): (1) the probes of one stretch depend on each other - in the hottest block (41) nine probes form chains of depth four (frame -> suspension -> application over it -> application over that) - so most of the chain is as long as before, whoever walks it first; (2) M1's profile was of v0; with \code{\verb~|code,casetrees~} the same corpus needs 5.6 times less CPU (M7: 8.79 -> 1.56 s), half the block-built keys exist already, and a hit is not one load but three (table, record, spine): "one cache miss per probe" is not the cost model of these blocks; (3) the root's code doubles (block 41: 4,638 -> 11,481 graph nodes, 31 -> 74 KB, measured with the checking counters compiled in).}

\p{\strong{Kept in the tree, off}, because the differential and equivalence gates ran over it and the next owner may want the predictor (\code{\verb~|aheadCheck~}) as an instrument; deleting it is \code{\verb~|Instr.*.ahead~}, \code{\verb~|BlockRoot.ahead / aheadInit / aheadKind / probes~}, the run-ahead block of \code{\verb~|T2Bridge~} and the four \code{\verb~|rlvm.t2.ahead*~} settings. It is NOT a candidate for the stepper either: the result above is an upper bound for a generic version of the same walk.}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Design finding: what a JIT for the checking process needs from the machine (for the owners, M1, M3, M6, M7)}

\p{The Truffle tier is correct and it is fed the right programs (the judgment chains of constants, owner note 5), and still there is almost nothing in them for a compiler to remove. The reason is the value model, not the tier:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Every intermediate value is a globally interned record} (I-ID: identity = structure). A block that builds a value pays a hash, a probe of a table far larger than the cache, and usually a new record and a new spine or frame run. On reachability that is 57\% of the warm time in the probes and inserts themselves (section 5); M1 measured that 96\% of the application probes of the generic tier are for NEW keys (15:58Z); of the records that reachability's blocks build, about half exist already (section 6) - either way the block cannot know without the probe. Tier equivalence (same records, same order) obliges compiled code to repeat every one of them. Escape analysis, scalar replacement, unboxing - the optimisations a JIT lives on - have no object to work on: nothing a block makes is ever private to it.}
\li{\strong{A block builds the redex and asks a generic judgment to reduce it.} \code{\verb~|MKAPP r7 (decEq Nat inst a b)~}, \code{\verb~|WHNF r8, r7~}: the callee is known when the block is elaborated, yet control reaches it through \code{\verb~|Reduce.whnf~} -> \code{\verb~|Rules.unfold~} -> \code{\verb~|Code.rules~} -> \code{\verb~|Code.enter~} -> the hook, with the arguments unpacked again from the record's spine. There is no call for Truffle to inline, so "one constant's code object inlined into its caller" cannot happen whatever the tier does. \code{\verb~|APPLY~} is the same through \code{\verb~|Eval.apply~}.}
\li{\strong{Arguments nobody inspects are built eagerly}: the motive and the \code{\verb~|List.below~} / \code{\verb~|PProd~} types of every structural-recursion step, the proof terms of \code{\verb~|Decidable.isTrue / isFalse~} (block 41 and \code{\verb~|instDecidableEqFin~} of section 5). In a strict machine they would be dead code; here they are the components of an interned record, so they must exist and be interned first.}
}

\p{What would unlock a compiler (and, each of them, also the stepper):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{U1 - call instructions.} \code{\verb~|CALL d, c, a1..an~} = WHNF of the application of constant \code{\verb~|c~} to the registers: enter \code{\verb~|c~}'s RULES / BODY block directly, intern the application only if the call is stuck or its result is to be tabled. One operation shared by the stepper and the tier, so tier equivalence is kept; the redex record, its spine run, the unpacking and the generic dispatch in between disappear for both, and the tier gets a call it can inline (\code{\verb~|DirectCallNode~}, T2's D5).}
\li{\strong{U2 - records that are not interned until they have to be} (M1's "young set", 15:58Z, is the table-side half of the same idea): a value made by a block lives in a per-activation or per-session nursery without a table entry and gets an identity only when it is stored into an interned record, used as a memo key, compared by identity or returned to a generic judgment. Then the values of point 3 are never interned, and in compiled code they are not even allocated. This changes I-ID from "always" to "once tenured" and therefore the tabling keys and the tick counts: it is a machine design decision, not a tier feature, and the equivalence rule between tiers has to become "same verdicts, same tenured records".}
\li{\strong{What does NOT work without them}: leaving the work and only overlapping its cache misses (section 6, measured: a loss).}
}

\p{Without U1 or U2 the upper bound of any compiled tier on closed evaluation is the stepper's own share (17\% on reachability; measured gain 4-10\%), and on library corpora (Cedar: 14\% of the time inside blocks at all) it is nothing or a loss.}

\p{\strong{CORRECTION (20:30Z, after U1 was prototyped and measured: section 10).} Point 2 describes the code correctly and its weight wrongly, and U1 does not pay. The redex of \code{\verb~|MKAPP ; WHNF~} is a memo key that hits: on reachability 77\% of those WHNFs are answered by the memo of the application's record, so "do not build the redex" means "compute it again" (ticks +8.3\%). With the record kept as the key (the tabled form) CALL changes no count by more than 0.3\% and no time outside the scatter, in the stepper and in the tier; on library corpora such sites are 1-3\% of the activations. Of the two unlocks only U2 is left, and the census of section 10.3 says where it can and cannot reach.}
}

