\subtree{
\title{10.5 The tier on top of CALL, and why the direct call node was not built}

\p{The task asked for "a direct call node so Truffle can inline callee blocks into callers". What exists is a node that reaches the callee through the shared operation (a boundary). Before building more I counted its object (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.t3.callCount=true~}, form T, production policy, one worker, per steady pass):}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{blocks installed}\th{compiled activations}\th{CALLs executed by compiled code}\th{callee's block entered as compiled code}\th{entered in the stepper}\th{no activation (memo answer, generic path)}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{13}\td{1,528,166}\td{461,720}\td{68,116}\td{21,374}\td{372,230}}
\tr{\td{Cedar slice}\td{2}\td{21,929}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
}

\p{So a direct call can serve 68 k calls per pass on reachability (4.5\% of the compiled activations) and none on the Cedar slice. What it would remove per call is the way from the node to the callee's root (\code{\verb~|T2Bridge.callv~} -> \code{\verb~|Code.callTabled~} -> \code{\verb~|callLoop~} -> \code{\verb~|Code.enter~} -> \code{\verb~|Tier.exec~} -> \code{\verb~|T2Tier.run~} -> \code{\verb~|callIndirect~}), not the key probe, the tick, the window, the callee's body, the WHNF of its result or the memo write. The hook's own frames were about 2\% of the samples of a Cedar-slice tier run in which each of the 600 k activations of a pass crosses it (section 9.3): some 40 ns per crossing. Taken generously as 150 ns per call (the hook, the boundary, the way through \code{\verb~|callTabled~} and \code{\verb~|callLoop~}), 68 k calls are 10 ms of a 920 ms pass, about 1\%, where two processes of one configuration differ by 5-8\%. And if those callee activations vanished entirely, bodies included, they are 4.5\% of the compiled activations of a pass: that share of the compiled part is the bound of inlining too, and inlining does not remove the bodies - what a callee body does is intern its results (section 5), which stays. I therefore stopped at the count. If somebody wants the node anyway, the place is \code{\verb~|Instr.Apply~} (\code{\verb~|call == true~}): the head is a \code{\verb~|KCONST~} of the same block, the callee root is known if it is installed when the caller is built (a later installation needs a rebuild of the caller: compiled blocks never run in the Truffle interpreter); the fast path is "key, memo miss, callable, tick, window, \code{\verb~|DirectCallNode.call~}", everything else (NONE, TCALL, over-application, signals, the WHNF of the result and the memo write) goes back to \code{\verb~|Code.callLoop~} at phase 1, which is already written to be re-entered with a block's result (it is how the frame \code{\verb~|K_CALL~} resumes).}
}

