\subtree{
\title{10.8 What follows from it}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Do not integrate \code{\verb~|call~}.} It is one more code path through the reduction core (about 440 added lines in \code{\verb~|Code~} and \code{\verb~|Elab~}) for no gain. The opcode 0x88 / 0x89 rows of ISA 4.1 can stay reserved or go. \strong{No variant of it can do much better}: the applications at CALL sites are 497 k of reachability's 8.4 M record-table probes per pass (6\%) and 92 k of its 4.8 M new records (1.9\%), and below 1\% of the records of a library corpus; that is the ceiling of "do not build the redex" however it is tabled. A side table keyed by head and arguments instead of the record would have to store the arguments to compare them - that is the record. The other shapes the task named do not have the redex to save: \code{\verb~|APPLY~} of a closure is already direct (beta without a record), the generic tier's EVAL builds the application because it is the value there, and CONV's lazy delta compares both sides by identity first, so it needs the records.}
\li{\strong{The WHNF memo of applications is doing the work on closed evaluation}: 77\% of the scrutinee reductions of reachability's case trees are memo answers. Anything that makes applications private or lazy (U2) has to keep exactly these as identified records - they are few (1-3\% of the activations of a library corpus) and they are the ones that are looked up again.}
\li{\strong{Where the records are} (census, 10.3): arguments and results of applications, and closures - values that are stored at once. For a compiler that means: with CALL or without, there is still nothing private to a block. The only remaining unlock of section 7 is U2, and on Cedar-level work even U2 reaches at most the 14\% of the records that blocks make unless the generic judgments use it too.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|t2~} stays off}; nothing in this task changes the verdict of section 1.}
}
}
}
